        <meta property="og:title" content="J Musculoskelet Sci Technol: Effects of Sit-to-Stand Exercise combined with Joint Mobilization with Movement on Lower Extremity Functions, Balance, and Gait Performance in Patients with Chronic Stroke">
        <meta property="og:url" content="http://www.jkema.org/archive/view_article?pid=jkema-9-1-87&amp;utm_source=chatgpt.com">
        <meta property="og:description" content="Background The range of motion of the ankle joint in stroke patients facilitates the smooth performance of functional movements and highlights the necessity of adequate ankle joint range of motion for performing these functional movements. Purpose The purpose of this study was to compare the effects of sit-to-stand exercises combined with joint mobilization with movements (MWM) and passive ankle stretching followed by sit-to-stand exercises on lower extremity functions, balance, and gait performance in stroke patients. Study design Two-group pre- and post-test design Methods A total of 34 chronic stroke patients were randomly divided into 2 groups. Sit-to-stand using MWM group (SUG) performed sit-to-stand exercises combined with MWM. Passive stretching group (PSG) performed passive ankle stretching followed by sit-to-stand exercises. A two-way ANOVA was used to examine the interactions between the two groups. The statistical significance level (&lt;italic&gt;Î±&lt;/italic&gt;) was set at 0.05. Results SUG showed statistically significant difference in the dorsiflexion active range of motion (AROM), the five time sit-to-stand (FTSTS) and the 10-meter walking (10MWT) (&lt;italic&gt;p&lt;/italic&gt; &amp;lt; 0.05). PSG showed statistically significant difference in the FTSTS and 10MWT (&lt;italic&gt;p&lt;/italic&gt; &amp;lt; 0.05). A post-hoc analysis was conducted to compare pre- and post-test outcomes between groups; however, no statistically significant differences were found in dorsiflexion AROM, the FTSTS and 10MWT. (&lt;italic&gt;p&lt;/italic&gt; &amp;gt;0.05). Conclusions Sit-to-stand exercise combined with MWM may be suggested as an effective program for improving lower extremity functions, balance, gait performance of stroke patients. ">
